 \section{Introduction}
 \label{sec:intro}
 %======================================================================
-Machine learning in medicine is usually told as a story of scale, but most clinical prediction tasks are not large. A hospital that wants to predict a complication from its own patients may have a few hundred records; a rare disease may have only a few dozen cases; and privacy rules usually prevent the hospital from combining its records with those of other institutions~\cite{simoes2026smalldata,rieke2020digitalhealth}. In such settings the data are \emph{small}: not merely few in absolute terms, but too few for the task that has to be learned from them.
+Many clinical prediction tasks suffer from a lack of data. A rare disease may have only a few dozen diagnosed cases, a study conducted in a single healthcare facility is limited to the patients it can recruit, and labelling records often requires expert review. Data sharing between institutions could increase the number of records, but it is frequently restricted by privacy regulations~\cite{rieke2020digitalhealth}. In this paper we refer to such datasets as \emph{small data}: datasets whose number of records is low relative to what the learning task requires, which depends on the number of features, the number of classes and the frequency of the outcome~\cite{riley2019binary}. A common way to mitigate small data is synthetic data generation (SDG), which adds artificial records that follow the distribution of the real ones to the training set~\cite{qian2023synthcity}.
 
-What counts as too few depends on the task. Three hundred patients can suffice to predict a common outcome from a handful of variables and fall far short for a rare outcome or many variables. Our recent survey~\cite{simoes2026smalldata} makes this intuition measurable, and we follow its definition throughout the paper. Every prediction task has a \emph{sample complexity} $n^{\star}_{T}$, the number of independent, correctly labelled records a model needs to reach a required error; for clinical models it can be estimated with the sample-size criteria of Riley et al.~\cite{riley2019binary}. Every dataset, in turn, has an \emph{effective sample size} $n_{\mathrm{eff}}(D)$, the number of such records it is worth once repeated measurements, missing labels, label noise and differences from the target population are accounted for. A dataset is small for its task when it is worth fewer records than the task needs, that is, when the ratio $\sigma_T(D)=n^{\star}_{T}/n_{\mathrm{eff}}(D)$, which the survey calls the \emph{smallness index}, exceeds one; ratios up to 10 are called strained, up to 100 small, and above 100 extreme. Small data is therefore a property of the whole dataset relative to its task, not of any single class: class imbalance matters only because, for a binary outcome, $n^{\star}_{T}$ grows with the rarity of the events~\cite{riley2019binary}. The six medical datasets we study illustrate the range. They have between 195 and 768 records, and their smallness indices run from 1.0 to 42.1 (Table~\ref{tab:datasets}), so each of them holds less evidence than its task requires.
+SDG is, however, hardest when data are small. Generators that learn the distribution from the real records, such as SMOTE~\cite{chawla2002smote}, generative adversarial networks (CTGAN) and variational autoencoders (TVAE)~\cite{xu2019ctgan}, must estimate it from the same few records they are meant to complement. They can only recombine patterns already present in those records, for example by interpolating between two existing patients, and cannot introduce knowledge that the records do not reflect~\cite{cover2006elements}. Large language models (LLMs) offer a different source of information. They are pre-trained on large text corpora, which gives them general knowledge about clinical variables, such as plausible ranges and typical relations between them, and they can generate new records from a few real examples placed in the prompt, without any training on the target dataset~\cite{borisov2023great,seedat2024cllm}. Their output, however, is free text rather than a dataset: a response may contain fewer records than requested, malformed records, missing or invented features, or values of the wrong type (Figure~\ref{fig:teaser}), and the cost of each request is usually not recorded. The problem addressed in this paper is therefore how to obtain from an LLM a synthetic dataset that has the requested size, conforms to the schema of the real data and has a known cost, and how to determine whether such a dataset improves classifiers trained on small data.
 
-How can a model be learned from such data? The survey classifies the possible remedies by where the missing information comes from: other institutions, related tasks or pre-trained models, domain knowledge, unlabelled data, new data collection, or the transformation of the existing data, which includes synthetic data generation (SDG)~\cite{simoes2026smalldata}. SDG is attractive because it adds training records without new collection and, when the synthetic records do not reproduce real ones, can support development and testing without exposing patients~\cite{qian2023synthcity}. The survey also sets a limit on what it can achieve. A synthetic dataset produced from $D$ with the help of an external resource $M$ carries no more information about the task than $D$ and $M$ together (Proposition~4.1 in~\cite{simoes2026smalldata}). A generator fitted only to $D$, such as SMOTE~\cite{chawla2002smote}, CTGAN or TVAE~\cite{xu2019ctgan}, has no such resource: it can help a learner use $D$ more efficiently, but it cannot supply what $D$ does not contain.
+Existing work does not solve this problem. Deep generators~\cite{xu2019ctgan,kotelnikov2023tabddpm,zhang2024tabsyn} and language models fine-tuned on the target data~\cite{borisov2023great,solatorio2023realtabformer} must be trained on the scarce records, and they were designed and evaluated on datasets with tens of thousands of records. Approaches that prompt an LLM directly, such as CLLM~\cite{seedat2024cllm} and EPIC~\cite{kim2024epic}, avoid training, but they focus on the design of the prompt and on the selection of the generated records; they do not ensure that the output is a valid dataset of the requested size, and they do not measure its cost. In addition, generators are usually compared on a single run, although in small data the variation between two runs of the same generator can be as large as the difference between two generators.
 
-Large language models (LLMs) are different, and this is why we turn to them. An LLM is not trained on $D$. It receives a few real records in its prompt and writes new ones using what it learned during pre-training, for example that \texttt{age} is a positive integer that tends to rise with blood pressure~\cite{borisov2023great,seedat2024cllm}. It is, in the terms of the survey, a generator that brings its own $M$. The difficulty is that an LLM response is a piece of text, not a dataset. Figure~\ref{fig:teaser} shows what can go wrong when a model is asked for 30 records: it may return fewer, add a feature that does not exist, omit one that does, or write a number as a word. Our experiments confirm that this is not a rare event. When we asked a commercial LLM directly for a fixed number of records, it returned exactly that number in none of 60 runs, delivering on average 87.6\% of it, and ten of its responses were not valid JSON at all (Section~\ref{sec:rq1}). Each request also costs tokens and time that are rarely reported. An LLM, in short, brings the right kind of information in an unreliable form.
+To address these limitations, we propose \system, a system that separates the generation of records, performed by the LLM, from their admission into the synthetic dataset, performed by a deterministic \emph{control layer}. The control layer selects the real records shown to the model, repairs and validates every response and asks for corrections, requests exactly the missing records, and logs the cost of every request. \system thus turns an LLM into a reliable source of synthetic data for small datasets without training a generator on them. This matters because it makes the knowledge of LLMs usable where established generators are weakest, and because a dataset with the requested size, a valid schema and a known cost can be audited like any other data source. The paper makes two contributions: the \system system, and an evaluation methodology for SDG in small data that pairs each classifier trained with synthetic records with the same classifier trained without them and measures the variation between repeated runs.
+
+We evaluated \system on six public medical datasets with 195 to 768 records, with four commercial LLMs, ten classifiers and ten data splits, against direct prompting and SMOTE, CTGAN and TVAE. The evaluation is organized around four research questions: whether the control layer makes the generated datasets reliable (RQ1), whether the generated records are faithful to the real data without reproducing them (RQ2), whether they improve classifiers trained on small data (RQ3), and how \system compares with other generators and depends on the LLM (RQ4). Direct prompting never returned the requested number of records, whereas \system, by construction, completed each dataset to the requested size for 1.9\% more tokens, with the same fidelity and utility. With the strongest LLM, the synthetic records increased the average F1-score by 1.09 percentage points and never decreased it by more than 0.53 points on any dataset, whereas SMOTE, CTGAN and TVAE decreased it by up to 8.2, 10.6 and 2.9 points. Only the choice of LLM changed the results by more than the variation between repeated runs.
+
+The remainder of the paper is organized as follows. Section~\ref{sec:related} reviews related work, Section~\ref{sec:problem} formalizes the problem, and Section~\ref{sec:system} describes \system. Section~\ref{sec:setup} presents the experimental setup, Section~\ref{sec:results} reports the results, Section~\ref{sec:discussion} discusses their implications and limitations, and Section~\ref{sec:conclusion} concludes the paper.
 
