+\section{Background and Related Work}
+\label{sec:related}
+%======================================================================
+To see where \system fits, we first review the families of tabular SDG methods, then examine what each family offers when data are small, and finally consider the work on validated output and on the evaluation of synthetic data on which our design and protocol build.
+
+\subsection{Synthetic Data Generation for Tabular Data}
+Tabular SDG methods fall into four families. \emph{Interpolation and statistical models} include SMOTE, which creates minority-class records on the segment between two neighbours~\cite{chawla2002smote}, and Bayesian networks, which have also been used for differentially private release~\cite{zhang2014privbayes}. \emph{Deep generative models} learn the joint distribution with a neural network: CTGAN and TVAE~\cite{xu2019ctgan}, diffusion models such as TabDDPM, CoDi and TabSyn~\cite{kotelnikov2023tabddpm,lee2023codi,zhang2024tabsyn}, and relational and cross-silo variants~\cite{fan2020relational,shankar2024silofuse}. \emph{Fine-tuned language models} serialize each record as text and fine-tune a language model on the target data, as in GReaT~\cite{borisov2023great}, REaLTabFormer~\cite{solatorio2023realtabformer} and TabLoft~\cite{chen2026tabloft}. \emph{Prompted LLMs} generate records from examples placed in the prompt, without training, as in CLLM~\cite{seedat2024cllm} and EPIC~\cite{kim2024epic}.
+
+\subsection{Synthetic Data Generation in Small Data}
+Most of these generators were designed and benchmarked on datasets with tens or hundreds of thousands of records, such as Adult (48,842 records) or Covertype (581,012 records)~\cite{xu2019ctgan}, and their behaviour on a few hundred records has received far less attention. Each family meets small data with a different limitation, and naming the limitations makes clear which ones a new system must address. Interpolation methods and deep generators are fitted only to $D$; by the data-processing inequality~\cite{cover2006elements} they cannot add information about the task beyond what $D$ contains, and they ignore what the features mean (L1, no external information). Deep generators and fine-tuned language models must also estimate many parameters from few records, so they can produce records outside the observed ranges and types, or reproduce training records~\cite{hyeong2022tabularmia} (L2, training on scarce records). Prompted LLMs avoid both problems, but nothing ensures that their output is a valid dataset of the requested size, and its cost is not measured (L3, unreliable output); CLLM~\cite{seedat2024cllm} filters generated records by their learning dynamics and EPIC~\cite{kim2024epic} designs balanced prompts, but both concern the quality of the records that are kept rather than the size, validity and cost of the output. Across all families, finally, generators are usually compared from single runs, so differences caused by run-to-run variation, which is large in small data, are reported as effects (L4, evaluation without a noise estimate). \system addresses L3 with its control layer and L4 with its evaluation protocol, and, because it uses a pre-trained LLM, it inherits the advantages of prompted LLMs over L1 and L2. Table~\ref{tab:positioning} summarizes these differences. The next subsection explains where the idea of a control layer comes from.
 
