-We set out to determine whether a control layer makes LLM-based SDG a reliable and useful option for small data, and the four answers combine into a clear picture. Reliability is achieved, and it is achieved by the control layer: the same LLM, prompted directly, never returned the dataset that was requested, whereas \system completes it by construction, records its cost and caught the failures of a less disciplined model, at almost no extra cost (RQ1). Usefulness is real but bounded: the synthetic records are faithful without reproducing real ones, yet distinguishable from them (RQ2); they help classifiers that fail on the minority class (RQ3); and with a stronger LLM they give the most consistent F1 gain among the generators compared (RQ4). No generator, however, improves utility significantly on all datasets. This is what our survey's conservation result predicts~\cite{simoes2026smalldata}: SDG cannot replace missing patients, and its benefit is bounded by the knowledge the generator brings. Among the generators we compared, the LLM is the only one that brings such knowledge, and the control layer is what makes it usable.
